1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,556
Walk into Singapore's Chinatown, and the shophouses are often
the first thing you notice.

2
00:00:08,256 --> 00:00:12,409
Yet the history of a street also lives in everyday encounters:

3
00:00:12,409 --> 00:00:18,079
buying food, running a business, sharing
skills, and returning to familiar places.

4
00:00:18,779 --> 00:00:22,895
When the buildings survive, what
helps these relationships continue?

5
00:00:23,595 --> 00:00:28,200
This film follows that question through the streets and one
working tea business.

6
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,456
Chinatown grew through migration, trade and settlement.

7
00:00:45,928 --> 00:00:49,505
Its shophouses connected work and home, with business on the

8
00:00:49,505 --> 00:00:51,679
ground floor and living space above.

9
00:00:52,151 --> 00:00:55,944
The historic district contains several different precincts.

10
00:00:56,416 --> 00:01:00,104
Here, a small selection of streets offers a starting point,

11
00:01:00,104 --> 00:01:03,423
rather than a complete picture of the whole district.

12
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,090
Remembering an earlier Chinatown does not mean that every part
of its past should return.

13
00:01:10,412 --> 00:01:14,099
Older housing also faced overcrowding and poor sanitation.

14
00:01:14,421 --> 00:01:16,712
People needed better living conditions.

15
00:01:17,034 --> 00:01:20,923
Conservation therefore involves choices about what to retain,

16
00:01:20,923 --> 00:01:24,597
what to improve, and whose needs should guide those changes.

17
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,903
On the seventh of July, nineteen eighty-nine, Chinatown
received conservation status.

18
00:01:32,153 --> 00:01:35,849
This helped protect its architectural
and historical character.

19
00:01:36,099 --> 00:01:39,172
But keeping a building and sustaining its use are related

20
00:01:39,172 --> 00:01:41,092
questions with different answers.

21
00:01:41,342 --> 00:01:44,917
A restored facade alone cannot
tell us whether people still have

22
00:01:44,917 --> 00:01:47,698
the conditions to live, work and gather around it.

23
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,261
Festivals make Chinatown's cultural
identity especially visible.

24
00:01:56,564 --> 00:02:00,231
Lights and markets bring people together, while exhibitions

25
00:02:00,231 --> 00:02:02,488
introduce visitors to earlier lives.

26
00:02:02,791 --> 00:02:05,333
These are valuable ways of sharing history.

27
00:02:05,636 --> 00:02:09,270
The festival footage here records Chinese New Year in twenty

28
00:02:09,270 --> 00:02:12,618
twenty-five, rather than an ordinary day on the street.

29
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,230
The rooms inside the Chinatown Heritage Centre are

30
00:02:17,230 --> 00:02:21,211
reconstructions, designed to help visitors imagine the past.

31
00:02:21,911 --> 00:02:24,962
They should not be mistaken for historical film.

32
00:02:25,662 --> 00:02:30,253
Alongside these displays, another
question remains: how do people

33
00:02:30,253 --> 00:02:34,119
use the district today, outside the moments selected for

34
00:02:34,119 --> 00:02:36,052
celebration or exhibition?

35
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,222
These street images show shops,
goods and people passing through.

36
00:02:42,922 --> 00:02:46,121
They cannot tell us who is a resident or a tourist, or

37
00:02:46,121 --> 00:02:48,204
how long a business has been here.

38
00:02:48,904 --> 00:02:53,048
They encourage a closer question:
what makes this a place people

39
00:02:53,048 --> 00:02:55,351
can keep using, as well as visiting?

40
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,939
Revitalisation can take different forms.

41
00:03:05,386 --> 00:03:09,822
In November twenty twenty-three,
CNA reported plans for a single

42
00:03:09,822 --> 00:03:14,499
master tenant to manage a row of
thirteen shophouses on Smith Street.

43
00:03:14,946 --> 00:03:18,673
The aim was to attract people while
retaining the area's heritage.

44
00:03:19,121 --> 00:03:23,457
Existing businesses also had concerns
about what change might bring.

45
00:03:25,250 --> 00:03:29,600
He wishes to stay on under the new master tenant

46
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:33,990
but hopes the rejuvenation project won't over-commercialise
the area and drive up rents.

47
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,515
A separate CNA report in May twenty twenty-five examined

48
00:03:39,515 --> 00:03:43,909
collaboration between businesses
and the Chinatown Heritage Centre.

49
00:03:44,494 --> 00:03:50,067
Some interviewed businesses described improved sales,
alongside continuing pressures.

50
00:03:50,651 --> 00:03:52,361
This was a different initiative.

51
00:03:52,945 --> 00:03:57,148
These reports cannot, by themselves,
show that the earlier tender

52
00:03:57,148 --> 00:03:59,325
caused those later improvements.

53
00:04:01,260 --> 00:04:03,540
First of all is definitely the rental.

54
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:08,560
Yeah, in this location, definitely it's not going to be low.

55
00:04:09,460 --> 00:04:15,920
And subsequently, I think for us, manpower is a challenge.

56
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,619
More customers can help a business survive.

57
00:04:21,319 --> 00:04:25,910
But sales, costs and the ability to
stay must be considered together.

58
00:04:26,610 --> 00:04:30,590
Shopkeepers are not necessarily long-term residents, and

59
00:04:30,590 --> 00:04:32,659
residents do not all run shops.

60
00:04:33,359 --> 00:04:37,438
A benefit for one group does not automatically reach everyone.

61
00:04:38,138 --> 00:04:42,266
The useful question is how tourism
income supports continued use,

62
00:04:42,266 --> 00:04:46,468
and which needs remain outside the
measures of commercial success.

63
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,917
Pek Sin Choon brings these questions
into a working tea business.

64
00:04:57,617 --> 00:05:00,282
My street footage records its shopfront.

65
00:05:00,982 --> 00:05:04,895
The interior scenes that follow come from a National Heritage

66
00:05:04,895 --> 00:05:07,605
Board film, published by Roots Singapore.

67
00:05:08,305 --> 00:05:12,688
They show a form of heritage
carried through practice: selecting,

68
00:05:12,688 --> 00:05:16,601
blending and tasting tea, handling materials, and passing

69
00:05:16,601 --> 00:05:18,479
experience to other people.

70
00:05:19,179 --> 00:05:24,135
The building matters, and so does the knowledge that people
continue to use inside it.

71
00:05:31,340 --> 00:05:35,900
These 100 years in Singapore, we have been experiencing tea,

72
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:39,260
drinking tea, sharing tea and making teas.

73
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:42,580
It's all inside our blood for generations of us here.

74
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,952
This is one way to understand living heritage.

75
00:05:48,652 --> 00:05:53,156
Continuity depends on people
continuing to do things, learn them,

76
00:05:53,156 --> 00:05:55,327
and adapt them to present needs.

77
00:05:56,027 --> 00:06:00,337
New workshops or partnerships do
not automatically weaken tradition.

78
00:06:01,037 --> 00:06:04,631
Their significance depends on
whether they support the knowledge,

79
00:06:04,631 --> 00:06:07,847
relationships and everyday work through which it survives.

80
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,177
A twenty twenty-six CNA report
also shows Pek Sin Choon sharing

81
00:06:15,177 --> 00:06:18,094
its stories through community programmes.

82
00:06:18,766 --> 00:06:21,563
These connections extend beyond the shopfront.

83
00:06:22,235 --> 00:06:26,673
They offer one example of how a business
can remain part of present life.

84
00:06:27,344 --> 00:06:30,969
The case does not represent every business or resident, and

85
00:06:30,969 --> 00:06:34,232
continued operation still needs practical support.

86
00:06:37,270 --> 00:06:42,030
Hopefully, Singapore will have more of the heritage businesses
that are here to last.

87
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,717
People can agree that Chinatown deserves protection and still

88
00:06:56,717 --> 00:06:58,681
disagree about how to protect it.

89
00:06:59,381 --> 00:07:03,540
A resident, a shopkeeper, a visitor
and a planning authority may

90
00:07:03,540 --> 00:07:06,466
value the same street for different reasons.

91
00:07:07,166 --> 00:07:09,018
These identities can overlap.

92
00:07:09,718 --> 00:07:15,129
The community is not a single voice, and
no one example can speak for everyone.

93
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:24,764
Public agencies, businesses and cultural
organisations can all provide resources.

94
00:07:25,014 --> 00:07:28,822
Their involvement also raises questions about priorities.

95
00:07:29,072 --> 00:07:32,941
Being invited to an activity, being asked for an opinion, and

96
00:07:32,941 --> 00:07:37,418
being able to change a decision are
different forms of participation.

97
00:07:37,668 --> 00:07:41,854
To understand conservation, we
need to examine whose preferences

98
00:07:41,854 --> 00:07:43,648
actually shape the choices.

99
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:49,192
Moving out of an old shophouse need
not end a connection with Chinatown.

100
00:07:49,892 --> 00:07:54,859
Returning to shop, work or take part
in religious life can also sustain it.

101
00:07:55,559 --> 00:07:59,955
These links deserve attention beyond
the streets most visible to visitors.

102
00:08:00,655 --> 00:08:05,286
The issue is how a conserved district
remains connected to the people who use it.

103
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,518
The next time you walk through
Chinatown, consider three questions.

104
00:08:18,218 --> 00:08:21,230
First, who can continue to use this place?

105
00:08:21,930 --> 00:08:26,250
Look beyond the appearance of the buildings
to the activities they make possible.

106
00:08:26,950 --> 00:08:30,621
A street can carry memory through repeated use, even as the

107
00:08:30,621 --> 00:08:32,793
people and their routines change.

108
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:39,008
Second, do the benefits help sustain work and transmission?

109
00:08:39,409 --> 00:08:43,420
Visitor numbers show attraction, but they do not explain every

110
00:08:43,420 --> 00:08:45,994
cost, or whether skills can be passed on.

111
00:08:46,394 --> 00:08:49,629
Third, can different users influence change?

112
00:08:50,030 --> 00:08:53,481
Conservation decisions should be examined through their

113
00:08:53,481 --> 00:08:56,510
consequences, as well as their stated intentions.

114
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:01,849
These materials lead me to
understand buildings, livelihoods and

115
00:09:01,849 --> 00:09:03,911
everyday relationships together.

116
00:09:04,256 --> 00:09:07,931
Tourism and cultural display can create opportunities.

117
00:09:08,276 --> 00:09:12,427
Their value also depends on the
conditions they leave for continued use.

118
00:09:12,772 --> 00:09:16,799
Conserving Chinatown should keep
history visible while allowing

119
00:09:16,799 --> 00:09:21,545
people today to practise it, learn from
it, and take part in shaping its future.
